1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,800
Which account is worth your next conversation, and why?

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,520
That is the job SalesPlay helps a seller work through.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:13,890
In this example, Jamie sells robotic core-stacking equipment to
transformer manufacturers in North

4
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:19,120
America. The goal is to find manufacturers that could need more core-
assembly capacity.

5
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,920
This walkthrough uses sample accounts and research.

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:27,820
The seller sets the outcome; agents connect market context, product fit,
and account evidence,

7
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:29,530
then prepare a useful next move.

8
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:36,760
SalesPlay starts a research plan. It checks the market context, narrows
the relevant manufacturing

9
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:42,530
process, investigates accounts beyond the existing list, and separates a
possible fit from a

10
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:47,010
confirmed need. Each completed step links to its evidence.

11
00:00:47,453 --> 00:00:52,230
MarketsandMarkets' specialist research could add value here by
connecting a demand signal to the

12
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:56,640
applications and processes a product can actually serve.

13
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:00,340
In this demonstration, that specialist map is simulated.

14
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:07,350
Growing transformer demand creates a reason to investigate; it does not
prove that any account needs Jamie's equipment.

15
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:10,860
Ridgeway appears as a new account.

16
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:17,240
Its starting profile suggests a possible fit, but one important question
is still open: does the

17
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:23,040
plant assemble laminated cores in-house? HarborGrid is already known to
Jamie and needs a

18
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:29,730
different expansion conversation. Summit is excluded because its wound-
core process does not fit

19
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:36,050
the equipment. Opening Ridgeway makes the connection clear: market
context, relevant production

20
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:39,520
process, account evidence, and the next buyer question.

21
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:44,370
The seller can inspect both the evidence and what it leaves unconfirmed.

22
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:49,000
The investigation becomes something Jamie can use.

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,400
SalesPlay prepares a short opening for the plant operations team,
together with three discovery

24
00:01:54,770 --> 00:01:59,160
questions. Jamie can edit the message and check it against the current
account facts before saving

25
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:05,420
it. Nothing is sent. This keeps the conversation specific without
assuming an equipment project

26
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:12,090
already exists. The saved play stays connected to the evidence behind
it, so later changes can trigger a fresh review.

27
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:19,480
Now the buyer adds a decisive fact: Ridgeway buys finished cores from a
supplier.

28
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:25,360
Core assembly is outsourced. SalesPlay stops that equipment approach and
changes the research

29
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:30,240
requirement. The next account needs explicit evidence of in-house
assembly.

30
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:35,520
It then investigates Evergreen, a different manufacturer with a stronger
process match.

31
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:41,000
The original Ridgeway message is kept as history, marked for review, and
blocked from export.

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,251
The goal continues, while the work and the recommendation change with
the facts.

33
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:49,160
Evergreen still has a research gap.

34
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:55,540
SalesPlay prepares a focused analyst question about the account's
product-segment relevance, with

35
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:58,160
the goal and evidence already attached.

36
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:03,360
The sample analyst finding supports that match, but the buyer must still
confirm the production

37
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:09,670
need. When the sample buyer confirms both in-house assembly and a
capacity need, the next action

38
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:15,570
becomes a technical assessment. An application engineer should establish
equipment compatibility

39
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:20,511
and installation requirements before anyone makes a benefit or
investment claim.

40
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:28,240
The same journey can frame a focused pilot: one product, one market, and
five accounts.

41
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:33,780
Plan a pilot captures the intended outcome and prepares a brief for a
scope discussion.

42
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:38,560
This demonstration saves that brief locally; it does not submit a
request.

43
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:44,070
A useful evaluation would ask whether the accounts are relevant and new,
whether the evidence is

44
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:49,340
correct, whether buyers confirm a need, and how much research effort is
required.

45
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:55,560
The outcome to test is simple: a better next customer conversation, with
a clear reason to have it.

